"They ask you about the spoils of war (anfal). Say: The spoils belong to Allah and the Messenger. That is, the authority to rule concerning the spoils belongs solely to Allah and the Prophet; no one else has a say or approval. So fear Allah, settle disputes among yourselves, and obey Allah and His Messenger, if you are truly believers.\n\nTrue believers are those whose hearts tremble when Allah is mentioned, whose faith increases when His verses are recited, and who put their trust solely in their Lord. They perform the prayer properly and spend out of what We have provided for them. It is they who are the true believers; for them are high ranks with their Lord, forgiveness, and a generous provision.\n\nWhen your Lord brought you out of your home for the truth, a group of the believers were reluctant. Even after the truth had become clear, they were arguing with you as if they were being driven toward death. Allah had promised you one of the two parties: either the caravan or the Quraysh army. You wished for the unarmed, weaker caravan, whereas Allah willed to establish the truth with His words and to cut off the root of the disbelievers.\n\nRemember when you implored your Lord for help, and He answered your prayer: 'I am helping you with a thousand angels following one another.' Allah made this only as glad tidings and that your hearts might find peace. Victory comes only from Allah. He granted you security by casting slumber over you, sent down rain from the sky to purify you and to remove the whispers of Satan from you, to strengthen your hearts, and to make your footing firm.\n\nYour Lord revealed to the angels: 'I am with you, so give steadfastness to the believers. I will cast terror into the hearts of the disbelievers, so strike above their necks and strike every fingertip of theirs.' That is because they opposed Allah and His Messenger. And whoever opposes Allah and His Messenger, let him know that Allah is severe in punishment. Taste this, then; and for the disbelievers is the punishment of the Fire.\n\nO you who believe! When you encounter the disbelievers marching together for battle, do not turn your backs to them. Whoever turns his back on that day, unless maneuvering for battle or joining another company, incurs the wrath of Allah, and his abode is Hell. It was not you who killed them, but Allah killed them. And when you threw, it was not you who threw, but Allah who threw. This was to test the believers with a good test. Indeed, Allah is All-Hearing, All-Knowing.\n\nIn these verses, it is described how, prior to the Battle of Badr, the polytheists asked Allah for victory (fath), yet the outcome turned against them. Clinging to the covering of the Kaaba, the polytheists had prayed for Him to help whichever of the two armies was superior. Their prayer was answered, but because their condition was not sound, the result turned in favor of the believers. In fact, this verse shows that the cause of the battle was the aggressive stance of the polytheists. If they abandon this desire—that is, if they cease opposing Allah and His Messenger—it is better for them. But if they return to the thought of war, Allah too will return, and their host, no matter how numerous, will be of no avail. Allah is with the believers, yet this companionship is not unconditional; it depends on complete faith and the fulfillment of necessary conditions.\n\nThen the believers are addressed: Obey Allah and His Messenger, and do not turn away from him while you hear. Do not be like those who say 'we hear' while they do not hear. For the worst of living creatures in the sight of Allah are the deaf and the mute who do not understand and do not reflect. What is meant here are those who have ears yet do not hear the truth, have tongues yet do not speak the truth, and have minds yet do not comprehend the truth. Such people are lower than harmful beasts in human form. One who is born deaf cannot speak because speech is learned through hearing. However, the deafness and muteness here pertain to senses deliberately closed against the truth; there is nothing of good in these people.\n\nIf Allah had recognized any good in them, He would surely have made them hear, but they did not listen to the voice of truth; thus there is no good in them. Even if Allah had made them hear, they would still have turned away and not benefited from what they heard. Here, one cannot say, 'Why did Allah not compel them?' because the matter pertains to will and partial choice (juz'i ikhtiyar); compulsion does not enter this realm. These people heed nothing other than compulsion, so do not be like them.\n\nO you who believe! Respond to Allah and the Messenger when the Prophet calls you to that which gives you life. This call is an invitation that brings you out from a state akin to death into a free and eternal life. Allah intervenes between a person and his heart, and you will surely be gathered unto Him. And guard against a tribulation (fitnah) that does not afflict only those among you who do wrong; it spreads and encompasses everyone. This tribulation arises from sins such as showing laxity in commanding good and forbidding evil. Like the act of someone drilling a hole in the bottom of a ship, this tribulation affects everyone aboard. Allah's punishment is severe; therefore protect yourselves from tribulation. Remember when you were a few, deemed weak and oppressed in the land, fearing that the people would abduct you. Allah gave you shelter, strengthened you with His aid, and provided you with pure sustenance so that you might be grateful.\n\nO you who believe! Do not betray Allah and the Messenger, lest you knowingly betray your own trusts. Your wealth and your children are nothing but a means of trial; and with Allah is a great reward. If you are mindful of Allah, He will grant you a criterion (furqan—an insight distinguishing truth from falsehood), expiate your bad deeds, and forgive you. Allah is the Possessor of great bounty. This 'furqan' makes you a radiant and enlightened community, like the dawn breaking in the darkness of night, covers your faults, and elevates your honor.\n\nRemember when the disbelievers plotted against you to restrain you, kill you, or expel you; Allah countered their plotting with the best of plots (the permission for the Hijrah). The polytheists gathered at Dar al-Nadwah and made plans to imprison, banish, or kill the Prophet (peace be upon him). At the proposal of Abu Jahl, they decided to choose a young man from every tribe to kill him collectively. When Gabriel informed him of the situation, our Prophet had Ali lie in his bed and took refuge in the cave with Abu Bakr. When they raided in the morning, they were astonished to see Ali.\n\nWhen the verses were recited to them, these disbelievers said, 'We have heard; if we wished, we could say the like of this; these are nothing but legends of the ancients.' They even challenged, 'O Allah, if this is indeed the truth from You, then rain down stones upon us from the sky or bring upon us a painful punishment.' Yet Allah was not going to punish them while you were among them, nor does He punish them while they seek forgiveness (istighfar). But now they prevent people from the Sacred Mosque (al-Masjid al-Haram), whereas only the righteous and God-fearing (al-muttaqun) are worthy of its custodianship. Their prayer at the Kaaba is nothing but whistling and clapping of hands. So taste the punishment because of your disbelief. Those who spend their wealth to hinder others from the path of Allah will continue to spend it; then it will become a source of regret for them, and they will be overcome. The disbelievers will be gathered and driven into Hell. Allah will separate the vile from the pure, heap the vile one upon another, and cast them all into Hell. Say to them: If they desist, what is past will be forgiven them; but if they return to rebellion, the precedent of earlier nations (sunnatullah) has already been established for them.\n\nThe polytheists devised all kinds of plots against the Prophet. Allah, in turn, countered their plots and foiled their plans. He gave them hope for a time and kept them occupied, and then, rendering all their efforts fruitless, He facilitated the Prophet's emigration (hijrah). At Badr as well, He misled them by making the Muslims appear few in their eyes; thus, Allah's scheming (makr) was intended to nullify the scheming of the schemers and punish them. The action of Allah is far removed from deception and trickery; His scheme serves to thwart the plotting of the plotters and bring about good. This expression is an instance of mushakalah (figurative correspondence), and scheming in the literal sense cannot be attributed to Allah.\n\nPolytheists such as Nadr ibn al-Harith denied the Quran when they heard it, saying, \"These are tales of the ancients.\" They even challenged, \"If this is indeed the truth from Allah, then rain down upon us stones from the sky or bring upon us a painful punishment.\" However, Allah did not punish them immediately because the Prophet was among them and there were those among them who repented and sought forgiveness (istighfar). The Prophet was the very embodiment of mercy; punishment would not be sent down upon a place where he was present. Furthermore, as long as there were people repenting and seeking forgiveness, punishment would not come. This is Allah's established way (sunnah).\n\nThe polytheists were barring people from al-Masjid al-Haram. Yet, they were not the guardians of al-Masjid al-Haram; its guardians are none but the righteous (muttaqin). What they performed beside the House of Allah in the name of prayer and worship was nothing more than whistling and clapping hands. In this manner, they amused themselves under the pretense of worshipping, and by making noise, they disturbed the peace of the Prophet. That they were not punished despite this was because the Prophet and those who repented were among them; it was not because they did not deserve punishment.\n\nThe disbelievers were spending their wealth to turn people away from the path of Allah. At Badr, they made a particular effort toward this. But in the end, this wealth will become a source of anguish and regret for them; it will go to waste, and ultimately they will be defeated and driven to Hell. Allah will thereby separate the wicked from the good, pile them all together, and heap them into Hell. It is they who are the true losers. Say to them, O Prophet: if they desist, what is past will be forgiven them; but if they return to fighting, they will perish in accordance with the precedent of the earlier peoples.\n\nAllah commanded fighting until fitnah (persecution and discord) is eliminated and religion belongs entirely to Allah. If the enemy desists, Allah sees what they do; if they do not desist, Allah is your protector. As for the spoils of war, know this: one-fifth of whatever spoils you take belongs to Allah, the Prophet, his relatives, orphans, the needy (miskin), and the stranded traveler. The remaining four-fifths are distributed among the warriors. This was revealed after the Battle of Badr. On that day, Allah aided the believers, granting them victory with a small force against a numerous enemy. The Prophet saw the enemy as few in a dream, which increased the courage of the believers. By causing both sides to see each other as few, Allah brought about the victory He had decreed. This is a miracle demonstrating that all matters are returned to Allah.\n\nO you who believe, when you encounter an enemy force in battle, stand firm and remember Allah much. This provides morale and leads to victory and divine reward. Even battle must not keep you from remembering Allah; for ultimately, all matters return to Him.\n\nIn the Battle of Badr, Allah brought about a wondrous victory by making both sides appear few and weak in each other's eyes. The believers took courage seeing the enemy as few, while the disbelievers initially underestimated the believers, and once the battle began, saw them as twice their own number. This is a miracle of Allah's power.\n\nBelievers must not dispute with one another; otherwise, they will fall into fear and lose their strength. Allah is with those who are patient. Do not go out to battle insolently and ostentatiously like the polytheists; Allah encompasses all that they do.\n\nSatan made their deeds attractive to the polytheists and said, \"No one can overcome you today, and I am your protector.\" But when the two sides came in sight of each other, he fled and said, \"I have nothing to do with you; I fear Allah.\" This demonstrates the ultimate outcome of arrogance and falsehood.\n\nThe hypocrites and those with disease in their hearts said of the believers, \"Their religion has deluded them.\" Yet whoever puts their trust in Allah, Allah is sufficient for them. Even if the believers are few in number, they prevail with the help of Allah.\n\nThe angels take the souls of the disbelievers, striking their faces and their backs, and saying, \"Taste the punishment of the Blazing Fire.\" This is the consequence of the sins their own hands have earned; Allah is never unjust to His servants.\n\nAllah does not alter a blessing He has bestowed upon a people unless they change what is in themselves. Pharaoh and earlier peoples were destroyed because they denied the verses and signs. This is a divine law.\n\nIn the sight of Allah, the worst of living creatures are those who persist in disbelief and repeatedly break their treaties. If you encounter them in war, punish them in such a way as to make an example of them for those behind them. If you fear treachery from a people, inform them openly that the treaty is terminated; indeed, Allah does not love the treacherous.\n\nThe disbelievers cannot escape or frustrate Allah. Prepare against them whatever force and warhorses you can muster. This will deter the enemies, and whatever you spend in the way of Allah will be paid back to you in full.\n\nIf the enemy inclines toward peace, you too incline toward peace and put your trust in Allah. He supports you with His help and with the believers. It is Allah who joined the hearts of the believers; even if you had spent everything on earth, you could not have united their hearts.\n\nAllah Almighty informs that the disbelievers should not think they have escaped or outstripped the truth. They can never frustrate Allah, the Prophet, or the people of truth and justice. Those who break their covenants cannot escape the punishment for their betrayal; they will certainly be seized. This warning recalls the state of the traitors mentioned in Surah al-Ma'idah, verse 13: because they broke their pledge, they were cursed and their hearts were hardened.\n\nThe believers are commanded to prepare against the enemy whatever force and tethered horses they can muster. This preparation is intended to deter and subdue both the polytheists of Mecca and other enemies whom they do not know. Whatever is spent in the way of Allah will be repaid in full, and no injustice will be done. Just as in the example of the two vineyards in Surah al-Kahf, verse 33, deeds will not go to waste.\n\nIf the enemy inclines toward peace, the believers should also incline toward peace. The primary objective is not war, but peace. Trust should be placed in Allah, and there should be no fear of schemes and deception. If the enemy intends to deceive, the help of Allah is sufficient. Indeed, Allah supported the Prophet with His aid and with the believers, and He bound their hearts together. While there was formerly enmity between the tribes of Aws and Khazraj, they became brothers through Islam.\n\nAllah is sufficient for the Prophet and the believers who follow him. While the believers were being encouraged to jihad, it was initially declared that twenty steadfast individuals could overcome two hundred disbelievers. Later, however, this burden was lightened; knowing there was weakness, it was stated that one hundred steadfast individuals could overcome two hundred, and a thousand could overcome two thousand. Allah is with those who are patient.\n\nIt is not fitting for any prophet to take captives until he has achieved a decisive victory and thoroughly subdued the enemy in the land. While taking captives and demanding ransom at Badr meant desiring worldly goods, Allah wills the Hereafter. Had there not been a prior decree from Allah, a severe punishment would have struck on account of taking this ransom. Yet Allah is Forgiving and Merciful; henceforth, the spoils of war may be consumed as lawful and good.\n\nThe captives should be told that if Allah finds any good in their hearts, He will grant them something better than what was taken from them and will forgive their sins. According to the narration of Abbas, he became a Muslim when he disclosed to the Prophet the gold he had secretly entrusted to his wife. If the captives act treacherously, they are doomed to be crushed, for they have already betrayed Allah before. Allah is All-Knowing and All-Wise.\n\nThose who believed, emigrated, and strove with their wealth and lives, along with those who sheltered and aided them, are allies of one another. As for the believers who have not yet emigrated, there is no bond of guardianship with them until they emigrate; however, if they seek help in matters of religion, assistance must be extended, provided it is not against a people with whom you have a treaty. The disbelievers are also allies of one another; if the believers do not ally with and support one another as well, discord will spread across the earth. Those who emigrate later are also from you; yet ties of kinship have greater priority in the Book of Allah.\n\nDuring the Battle of Badr, Abbas was also among the captives. He had twenty uqiyyahs (eight hundred dirhams) of gold with him, which he had brought to feed the polytheist soldiers. Abbas claimed that he had already accepted Islam beforehand, but had joined the battle under compulsion. The Prophet (peace be upon him) said that if what he said was true, Allah would grant him his reward, but outward appearances were against them. After paying the ransom, Abbas asked for the return of the gold, but the Prophet refused, saying, \"Do you ask for what you took to use against us? No, by no means.\"\n\nThe Prophet ordered Abbas to also pay the ransom for his two nephews, Aqil and Nawfal. When Abbas said, \"You have reduced me to a state where I will have to beg from the Quraysh,\" the Messenger of Allah reminded him of the gold he had secretly entrusted to his wife Umm al-Fadl when departing Mecca: \"Where is that gold?\" Abbas asked in astonishment, \"How do you know?\" When the Prophet said, \"My Lord informed me,\" Abbas immediately recited the testimony of faith and believed with sincere conviction.\n\nAbbas later said: \"Allah gave me something better in place of that gold. Now I have twenty enslaved servants, the least of whom conducts business in partnership with twenty thousand. Moreover, Allah bestowed Zamzam upon me, in exchange for which I would not take all the wealth of Mecca. From now on, I continually look forward to the forgiveness of my Lord.\"\n\nIn the 72nd verse, it is stated that the believing Emigrants (Muhajirun) and Helpers (Ansar) are guardians and allies of one another. They inherit from one another and manage each other's affairs. As for the believers who have not yet emigrated, there is no right of guardianship until they emigrate. However, if they seek help concerning religion, it is an obligation to assist them, provided it is not against a people with whom you have a treaty.\n\nIn the 73rd verse, it is emphasized that the disbelievers are allies of one another, and that neither guardianship nor inheritance exists between a believer and a disbeliever. Difference of religion and difference of abodes preclude inheritance. If these principles are not observed, great discord and corruption will prevail on earth. Believers are divided into the Emigrants (Muhajirun), the Helpers (Ansar), and other Muslims, and the principles of mutual solidarity among them are established.\n\nIn the 74th verse, those who believed, emigrated, strove in the cause of Allah, and demonstrated patience and endurance, together with the Helpers (Ansar) who sheltered and aided them, are described as true believers. For them is forgiveness and an honorable provision. The expression here, 'those are the true believers in all truth,' is a relative specification in comparison to those who did not emigrate.\n\nIn the 75th verse, it is stated that those who believe and emigrate hereafter and participate in striving alongside you are also of you. However, from now on, blood relatives are closer to one another in inheritance according to the Book of Allah. The provisions regarding inheritance previously established under the brotherhood pact between the Emigrants (Muhajirun) and the Helpers (Ansar) were abrogated. According to the soundest view, this ruling took effect after Hudaybiyyah. Allah knows all things."